Wild pointer — a pointer that has been declared but never initialized. It holds whatever garbage value was in that memory location. It is not null, not previously valid — it is purely random.


int* wild;          // wild pointer   — uninitialized, points anywhere
int* dangling;      // after:
int x = 5;
dangling = &x;      // valid
// x goes out of scope → dangling pointer

int* null = nullptr; // null pointer